StFttDbMaker/macro/Geometry -- FTT (sTGC) geometry (survey/alignment) tables
============================================================================

  ftt/*.C                 the Geometry/ftt survey tables, one file per
                          (table, beginTime) pair, in St_db_Maker's
                          <table>.<yyyymmdd>.<hhmmss>.C naming
  fttGeometry_db.C        reads those files and pushes them to the offline MySQL DB

These three tables are chained by StFttDb::loadGeometry() to place every sTGC
pentagon:

      stgcOnTpc           sTGC global position inside the TPC frame   (1 row)
      stationOnStgc       each of the 4 stations on the sTGC          (4 rows)
      pentOnStation       each pentagon on its station                (16 rows)

NAMESPACE: the DB namespace is Geometry/ftt. It used to be Geometry/stgc; the
rename is PR #20 (the StgmGeo1.xml <Misalign> paths) plus #23 (the
StFttDb::loadGeometry() half), which go together. Table NAMES did not change --
stgcOnTpc and stationOnStgc keep their stgc-flavoured names on purpose, because
only the namespace moved. Dmitry: "stgc is not a DB namespace".

The dates and times follow Gene's rule : https://www.star.bnl.gov/protected/spin/akio/fcs/database_timestamp.txt

      2021-10-10 00:00:00   Run 22 ideal     identity, all three tables
      2021-11-12 00:00:00   Run 22 iter 1    per-quadrant x/y from FST->sTGC residuals
      2021-11-12 00:00:02   Run 22 iter 2    + per-quadrant z (t2); x/y as iter 1
      2021-11-12 00:00:03   Run 22 iter 3    + measured deltas <-FINAL for Run22
      2023-04-10 00:00:00   Run 23 ideal     symlink to 2021-10-10
      ...
      2023-12-10 00:00:00   Run 24 ideal     symlink to 2021-10-10
      2023-12-15 00:00:00   Run 24 estimate  Run 22 finals copied verbatim
      2023-12-15 00:00:01   Run 24 iter 1    first actual Run 24 measurement
      ...

The Run 22 iterations sit in the "real data" range, which is where measured
constants belong, so 2021-11-12 is already right under Gene's rule. Their file
stamps are also wired into build_geom_misalign.C, the howto, several condor
submit files and three fGeom caches, so they are not renumbered.

EMPTY ON PURPOSE: the Run 23 and Run 25 survey slots (2023-04-15, 2024-12-15)
and the Run 25 ideal slot (2024-12-10). FTT has no survey-only table, so
filling them is a physics decision about what those runs reconstruct with --
Run 23 currently resolves to identity.

AN UNPAIRED "IDEAL" ENTRY HIJACKS ITS YEAR. Resolution is "latest beginTime at
or before the request", so an ideal entry in a year with nothing later silently
becomes that year's geometry. Measured: adding a Run 25 ideal link at
2024-12-10 took Run 25 from the Run 24 alignment to ZEROS, because 2024-12-10
is later than the Run 24 entries at 2023-12-15. Only ever add ideal and
estimate slots as a PAIR, and check all four run years afterwards.

THE THREE TABLES DO NOT SHARE EVERY STAMP. pentOnStation was revised on its own
several times, so fttGeometry_db.C carries a stamp PER TABLE -- the latest at or
before the tag's time, which is what St_db_Maker resolves to -- and always
writes all three together. Pushing pentOnStation alone would leave the other two
behind.

PER-QUADRANT z NEEDS THE FLOAT-ZPLANE GEOMETRY. From iteration 2 onward
pentOnStation carries a non-zero t2, measured against a geometry built with
zplane as float (PR #20). Against the old int-truncated zplane those z terms
reference the wrong plane positions.

A NEW ALIGNMENT GETS A NEW ENTRY -- never edit these. Step the time of day on
the date for that run year, as the Run 22 and Run 24 iterations do.

The DB is the truth. These files are the record of what was pushed, and the
way to override it locally.


Using these tables locally instead of the database
--------------------------------------------------
To MODIFY the geometry table - try an alignment, test a shift, reproduce an old
iteration, etc - put the tables in your working directory and tell the BFC chain 
to force local-only with two BFC options:

      NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb

NoMySQLDb drops MySQL and NoStarCintDb drops $STAR/StarDb, leaving your own
$PWD/StarDb as the only place tables come from. Your files then win outright
and you never have to think about timestamps. Set-up:

      mkdir -p ./StarDb/Geometry
      cp -r $STAR/StRoot/StFttDbMaker/macro/Geometry/ftt ./StarDb/Geometry/ftt
      Then add new .C files with newer date and time

then run the chain with NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb among its options, and a date & time
later than your new files. This is the right mode for alignment work and for any 
test of local table files.

The four options, from BigFullChain.h:1159 (StBFChain.cxx:263-268 empties the
matching directory out of the list):

      NoMySQLDb        drop MySQL
      NoStarCintDb     drop $STAR/StarDb
      NoLocalCintDb    drop $PWD/StarDb
      NoCintDb         drop BOTH $STAR/StarDb and $PWD/StarDb -> MySQL only

  Note NoStarCintDb does not clear $STAR/.$STAR_HOST_SYS/obj/StarDb, which
  stays in the list. That is harmless for us today -- the release has no
  Geometry/ tables there -- but it is not literally "nothing from $STAR".

  WITHOUT those options, ./StarDb does NOT simply win, and this is the most
  misunderstood thing here, so it is measured and quoted rather than asserted:

    * all .C files from EVERY search directory are pooled, and
      St_db_Maker::FindLeft() takes the one whose timestamp is LATEST at or
      before the requested time. Which directory it came from is irrelevant.
    * where a file and a MySQL row both exist, St_db_Maker.cxx:827 decides:
          case 3:   // Both SQL and CINT objects
            if (valsCINT[0].Get()>=valsSQL[0].Get()) { kase = 2; ... } // file
            else                                     { kase = 1; ... } // MySQL
      again the LATER beginTime wins. Files do not categorically beat the DB.
    * the list StBFChain builds is MySQL, $STAR/StarDb, $STAR/obj/StarDb,
      $PWD/StarDb, $PWD/obj/StarDb -- $PWD/StarDb is FOURTH. Position in that
      list is not precedence.

    If you cannot pass the options, the other way out is a timestamp later than
    every other entry: copy the file you want to <table>.20241216.000000.C in
    ./StarDb and edit that.

  * REMEMBER TO REMOVE IT when you want the database values back.

  * CONDOR JOBS run in their own scratch directory, so ./StarDb must exist
    THERE, not where you submitted from. Copy or link the directory into the
    job's working/tmp directory too, and pass the same options, or the job
    quietly uses something else and your test shows no change.

The "misalign" BFC option is what makes any of this take effect
---------------------------------------------------------------
The AGML/XML geometry reads these survey tables ONLY when the bfc "misalign"
option is on. Its entry in BigFullChain.h is

    {"misalign", "", "", "", "", "-AgMLideal", "Misaligned Geometry", kFALSE},

i.e. it works by taking AgMLideal OUT of the chain, which is what lets
AgPosition apply the <Misalign> entries in the geometry XML. Without it the
tables are ignored completely and the geometry is built ideal, however
carefully you edited them -- no warning, no log line, the numbers simply do not
matter.

With "misalign" on, these tables feed BOTH of:

  * fGeom.root generation. build_geom_misalign.C passes misalign=1, so the
    cached geometry is built FROM these tables. The cache is a frozen snapshot:
    once built it no longer consults the tables at all.

  * MC / simulation. The simulated detector is placed with these same
    parameters, so changing a table changes where simulated hits land, not just
    how real hits are reconstructed.

COPYING IS AS DANGEROUS AS LINKING if you forget to remove. Check your logfile
to make sure you are picking up correct tables from correct place. And be equally 
careful about WHICH fGeom.root you are using.

Pushing to the database
-----------------------
      root4star -b -q 'fttGeometry_db.C("run22iter3")'            dry run
      root4star -b -q 'fttGeometry_db.C("run22iter3","writedb")'  store one slot
      root4star -b -q 'fttGeometry_db.C("all","writedb")'         store every slot

Dry run first -- it prints every row it would write and touches nothing. Pass a
bad tag to list them all. "all" walks the eight slots in order.

Tags: run22ideal run22iter1 run22iter2 run22iter3
      run23ideal run24ideal run24seed run24iter1

An optional third argument restricts the push to ONE of the three tables:

      root4star -b -q 'fttGeometry_db.C("run22ideal","writedb","stgcOnTpc")'

That exists only for a first push, to test write permission on the smallest
possible change. A partial update leaves the geometry inconsistent at that
timestamp -- StFttDb::loadGeometry() chains all three -- so finish the other two
straight away.

Writing needs INSERT permission on Geometry_ftt, granted per account -- ask
Dmitry Arkhipkin <arkhipkin@bnl.gov>. Reads work without it, so a clean dry run
says nothing about whether you can push.

The three tables do not exist in Geometry_ftt yet; Dmitry is adding them. Until
they do, a push fails with "Table ... doesn't exist" and the macro says
NOTHING WAS STORED rather than claiming success.

NEVER STORE THE SAME (table, beginTime) TWICE. The primary key is
(nodeID, elementID, beginTime, flavor, deactive), so a second store at the same
beginTime collides with the first. A new entry gets a new time of day, as in
the table at the top.

PUSHING IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. St_db_Maker pools every candidate and takes
the LATEST beginTime at or before the requested time, whichever source it came
from, so files do not lose to the database by being files. $STAR/StarDb holds
three files under Geometry/stgc at 20221220.000000. After the Geometry/stgc ->
Geometry/ftt rename nothing looks there any more, and all three are identity in
every row, so they are dead weight; they are removed in the same PR that adds
this directory.
